{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Atlanta Braves arrived at SunTrust Park — their gleaming new ballpark — carrying more hope than expectation. A young roster built around Freddie Freeman, Dansby Swanson, and a collection of veterans was never projected to contend, but the early weeks offered tantalizing glimpses of what the rebuild might eventually become. Matt Kemp's sharp double on April 5th, Dansby Swanson's clutch single, and Brandon Phillips going deep suggested the offense could be scrappy and dangerous. But the bullpen — particularly Jim Johnson — was already undermining those moments, surrendering late leads that turned winnable games into losses. April's 10-13 record told the story accurately: a team still learning how to win.\n\nMay brought more of the same frustration, with Johnson repeatedly torching leads and opposing pitchers exploiting a rotation that lacked a true ace. Yet June ignited something unexpected. The Braves caught fire, going 16-12 — their only winning month of the season — fueled by timely home runs from Tyler Flowers and Matt Adams, and clutch doubles from Nick Markakis. For a few weeks in the middle of summer, the new ballpark buzzed with genuine excitement, and the young pieces looked like they might be arriving ahead of schedule.\n\nJuly and August brought the inevitable regression. The wins dried up and the losses piled on, with opponents like the A's, Phillies, and Rockies delivering crushing late blows. Arodys Vizcaíno, pressed into high-leverage situations, gave up multiple walk-off or lead-changing home runs, and the team's 10-15 July and 11-17 August records reflected a club running out of steam. The pitching staff's 4.72 ERA was damaging enough, but the bullpen's inability to protect close games was the season's defining wound.\n\nSeptember offered little but audition tape for the next wave. Unheralded contributors like Rio Ruiz, Lane Adams, and Johan Camargo delivered bright moments, hinting at the depth Atlanta's front office was quietly assembling. A 72-90 finish was disappointing in the standings but clarifying in purpose — this was a franchise in transition, planting seeds rather than harvesting them. The 2017 Braves were not a playoff team, but they were becoming the foundation of one.",
